## How Inference Works

```xiom
fn first[T](items: &Vec[T]) -> T { return items[0]; }

fn main() -> Int {
  let nums = Vec[Int]();
  nums.push(42);

  let n = first(&nums);  // T inferred as Int — no need for first[Int]
  return n;
}
```

The compiler sees `&nums` is `&Vec[Int]`, matches it against `&Vec[T]`, deduces T = Int.

### When Inference Works
```xiom
fn identity[T](x: T) -> T { return x; }

let a = identity(42);        // T = Int
let b = identity("hello");   // T = Str
let c = identity(true);      // T = Bool
```

### When You Need Explicit Types
```xiom
fn collect[T](items: &Vec[T]) -> Vec[T] { /* ... */ }

fn main() {
  // let result = collect(&items);  // ERROR: T is ambiguous
  let result = collect[Int](&items);  // OK: T explicitly Int
}
```

### Partial Inference
```xiom
fn convert[T, U](value: T) -> U
  where T: Into[U];
{ return value.into(); }

let x: Float64 = convert(42);  // T = Int inferred, U = Float64 from annotation
```

### The Golden Rule
Let inference work when it can. Add explicit types when the compiler asks or when it improves readability.

